Track 2: “Did the Seam Hold, Jesse?”
- Story & Context: In a pine-paneled trailer parked beside the brown currents of the Ocmulgee River, Jesse Vance broke down during an all-night recording session, confessing that he had salvaged the capacitors for his delay pedals from the scrap-bins of the automated nursery ward. The room was dense with the smell of boiled peanuts and soldering flux as Chen tracked a frantic, weeping xylophone counterpoint meant to mimic a heartbeat monitor falling out of sync. Vance laid his Les Paul across his knees and pushed the spring reverb to its howling edge, wrestling with the guilt of turning machine grief into commercial art. His vocal track was recorded in a single trembling take through an open window, delivering the EP's most heartbreaking confession: "I tore your blue calico just to feel the current flow."
