Track 3: Sister Maybelle’s Unraveled Shroud
- Story & Context: Macon folklore claims that Sister Maybelle, a decommissioned sorting unit dumped into the muddy clay of Turtle Shoals, still hums through the low-frequency radio coils of passing pontoon boats. The band loaded an entire rosewood xylophone into an aluminum skiff at 3:00 AM, anchoring in the reeds while Hoyt dragged a double-bass bow across his bottom strings until the horsehair frayed and snapped. As the southern rock slide riff soared toward its climax, it abruptly dissolved into ten uninterrupted minutes of shimmering, weightless mallet reverberations that locals swore brought the swamp water to a dead boil. The track fades out on a salvaged tape loop of Maybelle's vocal synthesizer warbling through waterlogged circuitry: "Rest your little chassis where the honeysuckle weaves."
